The buggy method is `com.google.javascript.jscomp.SourceFile.getLine(int)`.

The failure mechanism is visible in the implementation at [SourceFile.java](/abs/path/???). The method correctly scans to the start of the requested line, but its final branch mishandles files whose last line has no trailing `\n`:

```java
if (js.indexOf('\n', pos) == -1) {
  // comment says:
  // 1. if pos is at EOF, return null
  // 2. otherwise return contents from pos to EOF
  return null;
} else {
  return js.substring(pos, js.indexOf('\n', pos));
}
```

That unconditional `return null` is wrong. For the last line of a file without a trailing newline, `js.indexOf('\n', pos) == -1` while `pos < js.length()`, so the method should return `js.substring(pos)`. Because it returns `null` instead:

- `JSCompilerSourceExcerptProviderTest.testExceptNoNewLine()` gets `null` instead of `"foo2:third line"`.
- `JsMessageExtractorTest.testSyntaxError1()` and `testSyntaxError2()` lose the source excerpt line in the formatted error message, so `"if (true) {}}\n"` is missing.

I also checked the call chain statically:
- `Compiler.getSourceLine(...)` just delegates to `SourceFile.getLine(...)`.
- `LightweightMessageFormatter.format(...)` only includes the source line when that result is non-null.

Dynamic verification did not reach the nominated method, so the final conclusion here is based on static analysis, but the code defect is direct and matches all three failures exactly.

